      "biography": "Lisa Loeb & Nine Stories was the performing name used by American singer-songwriter Lisa Loeb and her band during the breakthrough phase of her career. Raised in Dallas after being born in Bethesda, Maryland, Loeb studied music, spent time at Berklee College of Music, and formed Nine Stories in 1990 with guitarist Tim Bright, drummer Jonathan Feinberg, and bassist Joe Quigley, taking the name from J. D. Salinger’s Nine Stories.\n\nThe group became widely known in 1994 with “Stay (I Missed You),” featured in Reality Bites, which became a major pop hit and made Loeb the first unsigned artist to reach No. 1 on the Billboard Hot 100. Their debut album Tails followed in 1995, and the group earned a Grammy nomination for Best Pop Performance by a Group.\n\nLisa Loeb & Nine Stories are associated with smart, melodic pop built around Loeb’s distinctive vocals and guitar-driven songwriting. Their early success established Loeb as a lasting pop presence, leading to later albums, continued touring, and a career that expanded into film, television, voice work, and children’s music.",
